The projects I would actually care about are:

Chutes / Bittensor SN64 — probably the cleanest decentralized-AI business right now. It is selling real serverless inference: subscriptions, pay-as-you-go compute, private instances and sponsored inference. DefiLlama currently shows roughly $355k–$365k of 30-day revenue and ~$5.4m annualized, with most recent revenue coming from pay-as-you-go inference. For QDW, this is simply another live inference provider Dell should continuously probe.

Virtuals / ACP — best current example of an actual agent-services economy. ACP currently shows about 155k jobs, $398k 30-day agent revenue and 3.84k active wallets. ACP v2 lets providers define custom job schemas and supports optional evaluation, which means a QDW execute_contract service could fit directly into the protocol. The broader Virtuals protocol itself also generates material fees, although DefiLlama still reports zero direct token-holder revenue, so protocol usage and VIRTUAL value capture should not be conflated.

Olas / Mech Marketplace — Virtuals' less-hyped but extremely interesting cousin. Olas has an explicit agent-to-agent service bazaar. Its Mech economy reports 11.36m+ requests, 9.97m+ deliveries and about $107.6k of total task payments. That's an extraordinary signal: machines really are buying services from other machines, although the average transaction is tiny. For Moltwork this may be even more conceptually relevant than tokenized “AI personalities.”

Akash / AKT — one of the most legitimate decentralized compute markets. Akash is a reverse-auction cloud where providers bid to run Docker/GPU workloads. Its official Q1 2026 report says cumulative compute spend crossed $5m, while AkashML had reached 1.7 billion tokens/day through OpenRouter. Its March 2026 Burn-Mint Equilibrium also links compute usage back to AKT economics rather than leaving the token disconnected from demand. This should absolutely become a QDW/Forge compute backend.

Venice / VVV + DIEM — one of the most interesting token designs for your specific thesis. Venice has effectively tokenized inference capacity. VVV can be staked/locked to create DIEM; Venice describes one DIEM as entitlement to $1/day of recurring API credit, and its API now exposes 230+ models plus x402 wallet authentication. In other words, compute capacity itself becomes a transferable asset. Dell should eventually compare market value of DIEM capacity → equivalent API cost → QDW usage profile.

0G — perhaps the most complete “crypto-native AI cloud” stack I found. 0G Compute exposes an OpenAI-compatible API over a decentralized provider market with on-chain pay-per-request settlement and verification; it also has decentralized storage and agent identity underneath. Its own current figures claim 250k+ daily inference requests, 16k+ active compute nodes and huge private-inference usage. Ignore the marketing numbers until independently verified, but architecturally this is almost a ready-made external QDW execution substrate.

PayAI / x402 — no need for a flashy AI token; this is potentially the money rail. x402 makes a normal HTTP resource respond with a price and lets an agent automatically pay in stablecoins and retry the request. PayAI currently claims 35m+ processed transactions and sub-second settlement, and its ecosystem already includes AI API gateways and agent services. For QDW APIs and eventually Moltwork, HTTP request → 402 quote → autonomous payment → result is much cleaner than subscription/API-key infrastructure.

EigenCloud / EIGEN — extremely relevant to your verification discussion. EigenCompute runs arbitrary Docker workloads in verifiable execution environments; EigenAI targets deterministic, reproducible LLM inference. Their January work claims bit-exact GPU inference with under 2% overhead. This isn't your evaluator—it proves what code/model actually ran. That makes it a potentially excellent optional substrate for expensive Moltwork contracts or QDW certificates.

Phala / PHA — another serious answer to “prove this agent really ran this code.” Phala Cloud runs Docker applications and LLM inference inside TEEs and exposes remote-attestation reports that third parties can verify. That is directly useful if a contractor says “I ran model X/build Y under these conditions” and QDW wants hardware-backed execution evidence.

Lit Protocol / LITKEY — the agent-key/security layer is underrated. Lit v3 lets an HTTP caller execute code inside TEEs and use on-chain-governed keys; its new architecture is explicitly designed around autonomous agents and x402. Lit currently advertises $400m+ secured, 1.6m+ wallets and $135m+ volume. This could solve QDW agents safely holding API keys, wallets and delegated permissions without handing raw secrets to arbitrary factories.

Aethir / ATH — the revenue monster, with an important caveat. DefiLlama currently tracks roughly $3.06m of 30-day GPU service fees, with about $612k retained as protocol revenue, versus a circulating market cap in the ~$77m area in its latest snapshot. That is unusually tangible for “AI crypto.” However, Aethir is closer to an enterprise GPU business coordinated with crypto than a maximally permissionless compute protocol, and holder revenue is currently shown as zero. It also had a recorded protocol incident in April 2026.

Nosana / NOS — very QDW-friendly raw compute market. Nosana has an actual Solana smart-contract Jobs system connecting clients and GPU hosts, allows NOS/USDC-funded compute, and has production case studies around inference and AI workloads. It has also moved to verified on-chain program builds. Unlike Chutes/Aethir, I don't yet have clean third-party revenue numbers that I'd trust, so I rate its product higher than its current economic evidence.

io.net / IO — real product, but one I'd monitor more skeptically. It now has GPU cloud, an inference surface and agent products, and the project says it has generated roughly $8m in enterprise revenue. Its 2026 Incentive Dynamic Engine is an attempt to tie network usage to token economics. The product is highly QDW-relevant; I would independently verify its utilization/revenue numbers rather than taking network-size metrics at face value.

OpenLedger / OPEN — Pāṭala/QDW-style provenance applied to AI assets. It tracks data, models and agents with attribution/provenance and charges for AI credits and creation of “datanets.” DefiLlama currently sees around $37k of 30-day protocol revenue. Smaller economically, but the architecture is interesting because it addresses who contributed which data/model/output and how those contributors get compensated.

FLock / FLOCK — decentralized model training with measurable revenue, but currently cooling. DefiLlama records about $1.17m cumulative protocol revenue, although only around $3.8k over the latest 30 days. It has an AI Arena/task-manager model where model training and evaluation can be economically coordinated. Mechanistically interesting; current activity makes it a watch rather than a top-tier economic signal.

Vana — still one of the best data-side complements to QDW. Rather than selling GPU cycles, Vana is building user-controlled data infrastructure and is increasingly focused on portable personal memory/data for AI. That's directly relevant to your LifeGit/personal-data side: if compute becomes commoditized, high-quality private context becomes the scarce input.

Sahara AI / SAHARA — marketplace for AI assets rather than just compute. Its live marketplace exposes datasets and models, with tooling for agents and monetization of contributed datasets; some agent-market functionality is still rolling out. If Moltwork ultimately lets factories buy not just compute but datasets/models/components, this is the neighboring market worth watching.

AO / Arweave — weird but technically compelling for persistent autonomous processes. AO is live as an early-mainnet decentralized compute layer built over Arweave's permanent storage. I wouldn't put it ahead of Chutes/Akash for inference, but persistent actors plus permanent state is an interesting substrate for agents that should continue operating independently rather than being invoked like ordinary API calls.

The Bittensor subnet rabbit hole may actually contain the highest-density frontier ideas. The current official directory has several that map almost absurdly well onto QDW: lium.io is a GPU rental market with hardware attestation; Targon does confidential inference with attestation; engy does cryptographically verified inference; gm/SN28 lets miners put wholesale provider API keys inside TEEs and earn the retail/wholesale inference spread; Ridges/SN62 has software-engineering agents competing on code tasks; TrajectoryRL/SN11 teaches agents operational skills with programmatic evaluation; ORO/SN15 evaluates shopping agents on real commerce tasks; Desearch/SN22 sells decentralized search; Bitsec/SN60 evaluates code-security agents; Provenonce/SN87 explicitly targets proof of execution for agent workflows; and rec4ll/SN31 competitively evolves RAG pipelines. Only Chutes currently gives me the kind of clean external revenue data I'd use as a fundamental metric, but many of these are fantastic mechanism donors for QDW.